## openarc.conf -- Hermes SEG runtime config.
##
## Generated by inc/arc_generate_config_file.cfm from this template.
## Three placeholder tokens (one for the operating mode, one for the
## AuthservID, one for the Domain/Selector/KeyFile triple) are substituted
## at Save time from system_settings + the active arc_sign row. The
## placeholder names are deliberately NOT documented here -- REReplace
## would otherwise replace them inside this comment too, corrupting the
## output. See inc/arc_generate_config_file.cfm for the placeholder spec.
##
## DO NOT edit /etc/openarc/openarc.conf by hand on the host. Changes
## are overwritten the next time an admin clicks Save in ARC Settings.
## To change defaults for NEW installs, edit this .HERMES template.

Mode                    THE-MODE
AuthservID              THE-AUTHSERV-ID

THE-SIGNING-IDENTITY

InternalHosts           /opt/hermes/arc/TrustedHosts
PeerList                /opt/hermes/arc/PeerList

Socket                  inet:8893
UserID                  openarc
PidFile                 /var/run/openarc/openarc.pid

Syslog                  Yes
SyslogFacility          mail
SoftwareHeader          Yes

Canonicalization        relaxed/relaxed
SignatureAlgorithm      rsa-sha256

## Disable the parent-directory safety walk on private key files.
## Our keys live under /opt/hermes/arc/keys/ which is a bind mount from the
## host. The parent /opt/hermes is owned by the host user (uid 1000), which
## OpenARC's RequireSafeKeys check flags as unsafe because that uid is not
## the openarc user nor root. In a containerized bind-mount deployment this
## check provides no real security (anyone with host shell access can read
## the keys regardless of in-container ownership), and produces noisy log
## warnings on every signing operation. Disabling matches what most
## docker-packaged OpenDKIM/OpenARC deployments do.
RequireSafeKeys         No

KeepTemporaryFiles      No
TemporaryDirectory      /var/tmp
